WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.045 --> 00:00:03.128
(anticipatory music)

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:07.200
- Hi, my name's Gordon
Bass, and I'm a writer,

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.520
and I want to tell you
a story about a man.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.330
His name was Ben Carlin,

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:13.250
and he drove the oceans of the world

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:14.710
in an amphibious vehicle.

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:18.113
There was only one problem:
the vehicle tended to sink.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:26.930
Ben Carlin met my grandfather in 1947,

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:29.350
so I grew up hearing stories about Ben.

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:32.110
Eventually, I became so
obsessed with his adventure

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:33.770
that I ended up writing a book about him.

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:35.760
Ben was born in Western Australia.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.130
He was a smart kid, but he
could never shake the feeling

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.510
that he'd grown up on the
very edge of the world,

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:42.650
and so as soon as he was
able, he wanted to set off

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:43.760
and find adventure.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.700
He'd started by mining for
gold in Western Australia,

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.120
didn't do very well at that.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.660
He joined the Army and
signed up for bomb disposal

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.540
in World War II, but he
ended up repairing latrines

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:56.190
and runways, so he felt
he'd been a failure at that.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.430
And finally, at the age
of 35, he decided he had

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:01.320
one more shot to accomplish something

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.090
that would put his name on the map.

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:05.140
He decided he was gonna circle the world

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.423
in an amphibious vehicle.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.180
The vehicle in question
was an amphibious version

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.140
of the World War II Army Jeep.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:14.600
So picture an Army Jeep
with a boat shell around it,

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.580
and that's what it was.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:19.610
Ben found one for $901
and spent the next year

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.330
preparing it for his journey.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:22.560
He called it the Half-Safe.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.650
It was supposed to be good on
land and water, but in reality

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:28.000
it turned out not to be
so great on either one.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:31.960
In 1948, Carlin and his
American wife Elinore

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.007
set off from Halifax, Nova Scotia.

00:01:34.007 --> 00:01:36.230
The journey was not smooth.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:38.820
The entire cabin was just 5 feet wide,

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.080
so the only way to sleep was to curl up

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:42.968
on the back bench seat.

00:01:42.968 --> 00:01:45.140
The engine was so loud
that they just stopped

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.030
trying to talk to each other.

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:49.570
They hit a gale in the
middle of the Atlantic.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:50.640
They thought they were gonna die,

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.580
and at that point they
realized they hadn't even

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:53.973
packed life jackets.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.330
So by the time they got to England,

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:59.670
they had to completely
rebuild the Half-Safe.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:01.830
They had less than $100 to their name.

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.700
So they ended up stopping for four years.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:07.030
Elinore got a job, and
Ben spent a lot of time

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:08.650
preparing the next leg of the journey.

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:10.970
In 1955 Ben and Elinore set off.

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:12.650
They thought it would
take a year, at most,

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:14.730
and they would be able to
return to their normal lives,

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.230
have kids, and start a family.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:19.970
When they hit the Middle
East and they were driving

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:22.880
through Syria and Iraq, Ben
discovered that he hadn't

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:24.610
ventilated the Jeep properly.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:28.970
It rose to 170 degrees, and
they ended up driving by night

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:30.980
and then sleeping in the
shade of the Jeep by day,

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:33.030
so they could avoid the heat of the sun.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.590
They were bandits, and
there were some roads

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:37.240
that they were crossing
that hadn't been traversed

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.990
by vehicles since the
middle of World War II,

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:41.570
which was 10 years earlier.

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:43.880
By Kolkata, the marriage was over.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:46.460
Elinore had had enough, and she left.

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:48.490
Ben continued alone over sea and land

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:50.080
for the rest of the journey.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.120
When he got to the States,
he'd hope for a movie deal

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.290
about his adventure, but
it never materialized.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:03:00.150
In the end, on May 13th, 1958,
Ben completed the journey.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:02.250
He traveled 40,000 miles over land

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:04.183
and 10,000 miles over water.

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:08.170
Part of the reason that Ben
never found lasting fame

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:10.700
was that there was no deeper
reason for the journey

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:12.503
other than to prove he could do it.

00:03:13.524 --> 00:03:16.990
This was the era before GPS,
and before satellite radio,

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:18.543
and before distress beacons.

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:22.970
So Ben was really navigating
just by the stars,

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:25.390
and he taught himself how
to navigate out of a book

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:28.160
that he bought in a
second-hand book store.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.620
What Ben accomplished,
going around the world

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:33.260
in an amphibious vehicle, it set a record

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:34.870
that stood for 60 years.

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:36.900
And not only has the
record stood for 60 years,

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.130
but nobody's even tried to replicate it,

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:41.180
which gives you a sense of the magnitude.

